der Cabinet Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Economy, Inclusive Growth and Environment Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-01","committee":"Prevention, Independence and Resilience Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Council","time":"5.00 pm","status":"New; Honorary Aldermen","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-23","committee":"Sustainable Future City Council Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-29","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-25","committee":"Cabinet","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Sustainable Future City Council Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.peterborough.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Peterborough faces three-year audit recovery, missing national target by a year","lead":"Peterborough will not receive an unqualified audit opinion\u2014a clean sign-off on its accounts\u2014before the 2028/29 financial year, the council's Audit Committee heard. The auditors, EY, assess that the 2025/26 accounts will remain disclaimed, meaning unaudited. The finding puts the council a year behind the national audit recovery programme, which aims to complete recovery by 2027/28 in all but exceptional circumstances. The delay reflects audit disclaimers on the 2024/25 Statement of Accounts, which highlighted weaknesses in financial reporting, supporting evidence and assurance arrangements. The council has recruited to its corporate finance team and engaged external advisors in specialist areas including capital, treasury and valuations. Two major balance sheet items\u2014the Revaluation Reserve and Capital Adjustment Account\u2014carry significant audit risk owing to concerns about historical fixed asset records.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Treasury Management","paper_url":"https://peterborough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60635/60635.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Peterborough faces \u00a37.9m overspend as children's care costs rise","lead":"Peterborough Council faces a \u00a37.9m overspend in the current financial year that will reduce its general reserves to \u00a36.7m, down from \u00a310.5m at the start, leaving the council with a minimal financial buffer. The largest pressure is children's residential placements, costing \u00a33.2m more than budgeted because average weekly costs have risen to \u00a39,000 from \u00a36,500. The number of placements has increased to 47 from a budgeted 41. The council also faces a \u00a32.7m income shortfall at Clare Lodge, a secure unit for children, which has been unable to operate at full occupancy due to restrictions imposed by the children's regulator OFSTED. Agency staff in children's social care are running \u00a30.6m over budget.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"June 26 BCR SFCC","paper_url":"https://peterborough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60459/60459.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Q1 Budgetary Control Report","paper_url":"https://peterborough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60951/60951.pdf","headline":"Clare Lodge secure unit occupancy recovery uncertain after OFSTED restrictions, with \u00a32.7m income at risk","detail":"Clare Lodge was under OFSTED admission restrictions, starting year at occupancy of 4 against budget assumption of 9.81. Restrictions now lifted but occupancy still only at 5. Recovery depends on plan to reach 10 by December 2026, reaching 11 in January-February. Success depends on recruitment of permanent staff to replace agency cover and maintaining OFSTED compliance. Failure to achieve occupancy plan would leave \u00a32.7m income pressure unresolved.","category":"demand","matters":"Service is dependent on regulatory compliance and staff recruitment; income forecasts rest on specific occupancy recovery plan that is not yet proven.","figures":"Occupancy down to 4-5 from budget of 9.81, \u00a32.7m income pressure if plan fails, plan requires 10+ occupancy by December 2026","quote":"Due to OFSTED admission restrictions, occupancy at the start of the financial year was 4 and remained at 4 for May and June. Restrictions have now been lifted and occupancy is slowly rising and is currently 5.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Q1 Budgetary Control Report","paper_url":"https://peterborough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60951/60951.pdf","headline":"Council funding capital scheme through borrowing to preserve reserves instead of drawing them down","detail":"Stanground Bypass improvement scheme (\u00a33.189m) was originally planned to be funded from earmarked reserves holding developer contribution. Council now proposes to add to capital programme and fund through prudential borrowing instead. Rationale: preserve cash-backed reserves 'at a time of sustained financial pressure and a forecast revenue overspend.' This reverses normal practice and adds c\u00a3400k annual borrowing costs (12-year asset life), but prioritises reserve retention over direct funding.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Council is incurring ongoing debt service costs to protect reserves, indicating reserves are now viewed as a critical buffer that cannot be deployed; this reversal of hierarchy signals financial stress.","figures":"\u00a33.189m scheme, c\u00a3400k annual borrowing cost, 12-year asset life","quote":"By replacing the use of reserve funding with borrowing, the Council can strengthen its reserves position, increase financial resilience and provide greater flexibility to manage emerging risks.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Q1 Budgetary Control Report","paper_url":"https://peterborough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60951/60951.pdf","headline":"37% of savings programme at high risk, with key schemes not started or dependent on decisions","detail":"At end Q1, 37% of savings rated red (7%) or amber (30%) for delivery risk. Highest-risk schemes include Children's Residential Homes (\u00a30.3m, not started, unlikely in-year due to lead time), Culture & Leisure (\u00a30.8m, dependent on operating model decisions with \u00a30.2m red and \u00a30.6m amber, value-for-money assessment due August), and P-Ltd Service Efficiencies (\u00a30.15m, dependent on identifying investment and capacity). All red-rated savings already reported as pressures in the \u00a37.9m overspend forecast.","category":"savings","matters":"High proportion of savings at delivery risk; if these fail to land, overspend will worsen from the already-significant \u00a37.9m position.","figures":"37% red/amber, Children's Residential \u00a30.3m, Culture & Leisure \u00a30.8m, P-Ltd \u00a30.15m","quote":"At the end of June, 37% of the savings are rated as red or amber and those with the greatest risk are summarised below: \u2022 Children's Residential Homes - \u00a30.3m \u2013 this programme hasn't started yet and unlikely to achieve in year due to the lead in time","material":false},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Q1 Budgetary Control Report","paper_url":"https://peterborough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60951/60951.pdf","headline":"DSG deficit projected at \u00a317.7m with government funding commitment for new deficits described only as 'appropriate and proportionate' and 'not unlimited'","detail":"Dedicated Schools Grant overspent by \u00a316.6m in 2026/27 due to increased demand for Education Health Care Plans, creating cumulative deficit of \u00a317.7m by year-end. DfE committed to fund 90% of deficits held at 31 March 2026, but for new deficits arising in 2026/27 and 2027/28 states only that approach will be 'appropriate and proportionate' but 'not unlimited.' From 2028/29 SEND spending to be covered in government DEL. The vagueness of this commitment creates unfunded liability risk.","category":"demand","matters":"Escalating DSG deficit creates long-term unfunded liability; government funding commitment is time-limited and vague, creating solvency risk beyond the current year.","figures":"\u00a316.6m overspend 2026/27, \u00a317.7m deficit by 31.03.27, 90% of historical deficit to be funded by DfE","quote":"For deficits that arise in 2026/27 and 2027/28, local authorities can expect that we will continue to take an appropriate and proportionate approach, though it will not be unlimited.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Q1 Budgetary Control Report","paper_url":"https://peterborough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60951/60951.pdf","headline":"Number and cost of children in residential placements both growing sharply","detail":"LAC numbers up from budgeted 429 to 444 (15 additional young people); residential placements up from 41 to 47 (6 additional); average weekly cost per placement increased 38% to \u00a39,000 from budgeted \u00a36,500. Nine placements now cost over \u00a310,000 per week. This generates \u00a35.2m overspend forecast and shows sustained demand-driven cost growth in an expensive service.","category":"demand","matters":"Demand for high-cost placements is growing and accelerating in cost, creating structural budget pressure that may persist beyond the current year.","figures":"15 additional LAC, 6 additional residential placements, cost per placement up to \u00a39,000/week from \u00a36,500, 9 placements over \u00a310,000/week, \u00a35.2m overspend","quote":"The average price has increased to an average of \u00a39,000 for the last 10 placements from the budgeted amount of \u00a36,500 a week.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-23"},"name":"Peterborough UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-24","since_iso":"2026-08-25","distress":true,"headline":"Council appoints two new Senior Auditors to strengthen internal audit function","lead":"Investment in internal audit function demonstrates commitment to governance improvement. Building internal audit capacity suggests confidence in remediating the identified control issues.","also":["44 other findings in this window","1 paper caught"],"watch":"Economy, Inclusive Growth and Environment Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-24","n_findings":45,"n_documents":1,"top_paper_title":"Appendix C: Internal Audit Progress Report","top_paper_url":"https://peterborough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60640/60640.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":166,"news_drafts_total":2}}